                "id": "part-1",
                "body": "How did the search for wealth by the Spanish and Portuguese monarchies trigger events ranging from demographic collapse to the formation of new racial classes?\n\nChristopher Colombus’ arrival to the Americas in 1492 is still considered as one of the most impactful events in world history. Although it provided many benefits (e.g. Amendment to the scarcity of foods in Europe, the collapse of ancient dogmas) and altered the political and economic balance of Europe. This discovery meant more than just geographical expansion. It also marked the beginning of a bloody, systematic colonization process that irreversibly transformed the continent’s millennia-old cultures, ecosystems, and demographic structure. \nThese discoveries led to a conflict over territorial claims between the two great naval powers of the time: Spain and Portugal. In 1494, Pope Alexander VI mediated the signing of the Treaty of Tordesillas to prevent a war between the two Catholic kingdoms. Yet, this diplomatic compromise did far more than avert a war; it gave us a really important answer about the linguistic question that was imposed upon an entire continent: why does an entire continent speak Spanish, while a single nation covering nearly half of it speaks Portuguese? The answer to this modern cultural divide does not lie in organic evolution, but in a meridian line drawn 370 fersahs west of the Cape Verde Islands, as established by the treaty. \nAccording to the Treaty, all lands discovered or to be discovered west of the line were assigned to Spain, and all lands east of the line were assigned to Portugal. At that time, the geographical boundaries of South America were not fully known, and the territory assigned to Portugal corresponded only to the eastern tip. This is why much of South America was shaped by Spanish culture, while Brazil was shaped by Portuguese culture.\n\n\nHow does this chain of events affect economics and politics up until today?\n\nThe history we told, right above, is already known by most of the population. But is it really sufficient to have learned only the parts that are enforced upon us in school textbooks?​​ The colonization period is far from being a bygone history; it fundamentally shapes both our present and our future.\n\nIf we consider that there’s so much importance on the emphasis of these effects, there may be two reasons that we don’t seem to tackle them sufficiently: We forget about these questions or we simply ignore them for the sake of the hierarchical order to which we are accustomed.\n\nWe believe that historical events affecting our future must not be neglected. So the aim of this project will be to address these questions we have been ignoring for a long time. To that end, everything we will carry out as part of the project will be as follows:\n\nProject Scheme\n\nThe Columbian Exchange\nTransatlantic Slave Trade\nThe social structure of postcolonial society and racial discriminations (the “casta system”)\nThe XVI Century Price Revolution, the Resource Curse, and the Triangular Trade Accumulation.\nEncomienda and Mita Systems \nPlantation Economy and Extractive vs. Inclusive Institutions\nCommodity Dependency\nCurrent Land Inequality & Gini Index\nThe Evolution of Risk Management: How Colonial Institutions Anchored Today's Global Capital and Derivative Markets\n20th-Century Sociological Studies on Postcolonialism\nSociological Facts and Today’s Latin America Laws and Constitutions \nRacial Stratification & Pigmentocracy\nCaudillismo & Clientelism\nSpatial Segregation & Favelas",
